# Deduction

**Deduction** is the inference from premises and rules to what must follow
from them. If the premises hold, the conclusion holds. In this vault it is the
second half of a cycle whose first half is [[induction]]: induction supplies a
model, and deduction unfolds the model into consequences that can be tested.
Deduction adds certainty and no new content; induction adds content and no
certainty.

The word covers two things that should be kept apart.

| | Deduction as a checked derivation | "Deduce" as a request to a model |
|---|---|---|
| Carried out by | a proof checker, a compiler, a program | a language model |
| Result | follows from the premises, verifiably | text that resembles a derivation |
| Guarantee | truth is preserved | none until checked |
| Card | this one | [[typed-transformation]] |

## Description

### Lane one: deduction is always done in the context of induction

Deduction works inside a fixed frame: a theory, a set of rules, a
specification. Inside that frame it is complete in its own way, since nothing
but the premises is needed. What it cannot do is supply the frame. The
premises of any deduction about the world were reached by induction or
[[abduction]], and the reason to deduce anything from them is to obtain
predictions that the next observations can confirm or refute. This is the
downward arc of the [[reasoning-cycle]].

The same arc appears in three places in the discussion.

- **Executing an established formalization.** A conventional program is
  deduction made mechanical: given inputs, rules and a model of computation,
  the result follows. Once a language model has helped establish the rules for
  deduplicating a table, an ordinary program applies them perfectly well. The
  useful line between programs and language models is the line between this
  arc and the other one, between executing a formalization and discovering or
  revising it.
- **Proof inside a theory.** In a formal theory, provability is deduction
  written down exactly. Arithmetic can even represent its own proof relation
  as a formula, which is what makes a [[godel-sentence]] possible. The
  [[incompleteness-theorems]] then mark the edge of this lane: a consistent,
  effectively axiomatized, sufficiently strong theory leaves some statements
  undecided, and no deduction inside it reaches them.
- **Resuming after an extension.** When a new axiom is adopted, the adoption
  is not a deduction, but everything after it is. Each stage of a
  [[reflection-progression]] is a deductive system; only the passage between
  stages is not.

[[mathematical-induction]] belongs to this lane. A proof by base case and step
is an ordinary derivation, certain in its conclusion, in spite of its name.

### Lane two: asking a model to deduce is not a checked deduction

A language model asked to deduce produces a sequence of steps that reads like
a derivation. Whether each step follows is a separate question that the output
does not answer. The discussion's caution is that the two must not be
confused, and its proposal is to model the operation with a type,

```text
Deduce: (Theory, Premises) -> Conclusions
```

and to attach to the result a proof that an independent checker accepts. A
compiler, a type checker or a proof assistant plays that role. Where such a
checker exists, lane two can be converted into lane one; where it does not,
the result remains a claim with a confidence.

## Relations

- It works only in a cycle with [[induction]] {relation: contrasts}: deduction unfolds what induction proposes.
- It is the downward arc of the [[reasoning-cycle]] {relation: part-of}.
- It contrasts with [[abduction]] {relation: contrasts}: from rule and case to result, against from result and rule to a possible case.
- [[mathematical-induction]] {relation: example-of} is a deductive method that carries a misleading name.
- Its formal limit is stated by the [[incompleteness-theorems]] {relation: uses}.
- It is what each stage of a [[reflection-progression]] {relation: part-of} does between extensions.
- It produces what [[prediction]] {relation: uses} needs: consequences of a model for cases not yet seen.
- It is one [[cognitive-operation]] {relation: instance-of}.
- As an operation offered by an agent it should be a [[typed-transformation]] {relation: is-a} with a proof requirement.
- It bears on the question [[ai-oriented-language]] {relation: answers}: executing a formalization is the deductive half of programming.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}, as one of the operations mapped there.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[induction]].** Inference from observations to a hypothesis that may be
  false. It supplies the premises deduction starts from and receives the
  observations deduction leads to.
- **[[reasoning-cycle]].** Observations, hypothesis, predictions, test,
  repeated. Deduction is the step from hypothesis to predictions.
- **[[incompleteness-theorems]].** Gödel's results that a consistent,
  effective, sufficiently strong theory cannot prove every truth of
  arithmetic. They locate the boundary of what deduction inside one theory
  can reach.
- **[[typed-transformation]].** An operation with a declared signature whose
  result carries evidence or proof. It is the device that separates a checked
  deduction from a model's imitation of one.
- **[[mathematical-induction]].** The proof scheme of base case and step. It
  is deduction along the natural numbers.

